Under “text-decoration-skip-ink: auto”, Ladybird draws underlines and
overlines through the glyphs of CJK text, and still skips them under
“all”. Blink does the same: Its ShapeResultBloberizer leaves out of
the intercepts every character that unicode::CanTextDecorationSkipInk()
rejects, such as CJK ideographs, symbols, and Hangul, but only for
“auto”. Gecko’s SkipInk() in nsCSSRendering.cpp passes over every
glyph run whose mIsCJK is set, again under “auto” alone. And WebKit’s
computeUnderlineType() in FontCascade.cpp draws through CJK characters
under “all” as well. Chrome 158, Firefox 151, and WebKit 26.5 draw the
underline of Han, kana, and Hangul unbroken, and skip the descenders of
the Latin text beside it; under “all”, Chrome and Firefox skip the CJK
glyphs too, while WebKit doesn’t.
